+**What `sourceInventory` contains (consumer24 A4).** The snapshot source inventory
+is (1) the deterministic custody walk of the admitted first-party files under the
+project root, which is every file the shared discovery rule reaches and never
+descends into a pruned tree (`node_modules`; `.git`, `.hg`, `.svn`, `.jj`; a Cargo
+`target` directly under a Cargo root; native §1.4 U-4a), **plus** (2) exactly the
+bytes the analysis actually required and read from inside the project root but
+outside that walk: files a Plan-selected TypeScript context read from an installed
+dependency tree it committed to its read set (`nodeModulesInReadSet`). Unread pruned
+material is excluded, so two hosts over the same checkout, analysis and read set mint
+the same `snapshot2` whatever else sits in `node_modules` or `target`. Required inputs
+read from **outside** the project root are not snapshot paths and are never flattened
+into this inventory: compiler files and the TypeScript standard library stay in their
+`toolchain` and `stdlib` closures, Rust dependency sources stay in their
+`DependencySourceSetV1` and closure records, and each is joined by its own owner. The
+retained `ResolvedNodeModulesLayoutV1` rows are package-directory resolution
+observations joined by blob digest, not inventory rows themselves; its registered
+schema description states the same law. Read pruned-tree bytes are retained
+under their inventory digests like every other row and appear in membership as
+`syntax-only` / `host-ignore-convention` (native U-4). Run closure joins them: a
+snapshot row inside a dependency tree must lie inside a package directory
+(`installPath` or `realPath`) of the retained `ResolvedNodeModulesLayoutV1` of a
+Plan-selected context that committed `nodeModulesInReadSet`, and a VCS-tree or Cargo
+build-output row is never a read input of any published universe. A dependency
+package's read allowance never overrides a VCS-tree exclusion deeper in that path;
+the VCS exclusion uses exact segments at every depth. **Nested packages have
+explicit custody.** A listed directory authorizes a read file below it only while
+the path suffix below that exact directory has no further exact `node_modules`
+segment. A nested installed package (for example
+`node_modules/left-pad/node_modules/evil`) is its own installed package directory
+and needs its own layout row; an enclosing listed directory never authorizes it.
+Listed directories are admitted `CanonicalPath` prefixes compared by exact segment,
+not package roots parsed from a path, so a package-manager store or workspace-link
+`realPath` authorizes its ordinary descendants, and a lookalike segment such as
+`node_modules-like` is no boundary. A row outside every pruned tree stays with the
+first-party custody walk even when a listed `realPath` contains it. Discovery's
+outermost pruned anchor is unchanged and is not this authorization boundary. Any
+violation refuses `SNAPSHOT_PRUNED_TREE_NOT_A_READ`. **What replay decides and
+what it does not prove.** Replay decides every inventoried row: a recorded read
+path that crosses a nested installed-package boundary without its own retained
+row refuses, whatever enclosing directory is listed. The custody walk and the read
+set are host TCB observations retained with the Run. Bytes-only replay re-checks
+every retained digest, and this join, at package-directory granularity. It cannot
+prove that the host walked every first-party file, that it read no file missing
+from the inventory, or which of a listed package's own files it read: those are
+properties of an unobserved host process, not of the retained bytes.
+Security S3 "Pruned trees and the read set" carries the matching custody statement.
